    <GaugingStation location="Darlington South Park" name="Skerne" number="25004">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Compound broad-crested weir with a fish pass. Flow regime influenced by reservoir, minewaters and sewage effluent.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Often out of bank, but the overbank flow passes over the two side weirs. At the highest levels bridge soffit influences upstream level. The weir and fish pass flows are combined (from 2015), and stage and rating only applies to the weir. One peak flow rating applied across period of record. Reasonable fit to available gaugings. Flood Studies Report grade B due to catchment changes (A2 for structure).</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Compound broad-crested non-standard weir, not purpose built but unlikely to drown. On low-lying ground, formerly the floor of a boating lake, under concrete foot bridge. Fish pass with a small Crump weir constructed winter 1995/1996 - acts as an orifice at high flows. Channel rehabilitation u/s to improve aquatic habitat and maintain low flows.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Site temporarily closed from June 2007 after being vandalised. Not reinstated until 29/09/2009. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in September 2019 (WINFAP Files v8).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Hurworth Burn reservoir (13.4 km2, 6% of catchment) in headwaters, no longer used for water supply. Pumped minewaters can augment flow, but this contribution declines over time. Excess flow from the Cocker Beck is diverted (u/s of Darlington) directly to the Tees. Significant sewage effluent component in low flows.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">Catchment underlain by highly permeable Magnesian Limestone, but nearly all the catchment overlain by superficial deposits, mainly Boulder Clay. Dominated by mixed agriculture, considerable urban development d/s in Darlington and Aycliffe. Mixed agricultural land use and significant floodplain drained fen peat (carrs) where overbank flood storage may attenuate extreme flood peaks.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">GEI</Comment>
        </Comments>
